n index of an $n$-vertex signed connected simple subcubic graph, other than $(K_4, -)$, is at most $\\frac{3n + 2}{8}$, and we characterize the family of signed graphs for which this bound is attained. This bound can be further improved to $\\frac{n}{3}$ for signed $2$-edge-connected simple subcubic graphs, with the exceptional signed graphs being characterized. As a corollary, every signed $2$-edge-connected simple cubic graph on at least $10$ vertices and with $m$ edges has its frustration index at most $\\frac{2}{9}m$, where the upper bound is tight as it is achieved by an infinite family of signed cubic graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeak-excedance mid-points and cycle descents — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/362029/weak-excedance-mid-points-and-cycle-descents)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Weak-excedance mid-points and cycle descents ... The number of weak excedances that are also mid-points of a decreasing subsequence of length $3$3 (Statistic $373$373) is equidistributed with the cycle descent number (Statistic $317$317) and thus exhibits the cyclic sieving phenomenon under involutions with $2^{n-1}$2 n−1 fixed points.\n\nWeak-excedance mid-points and cycle descents\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $S_n$S n​ be the set of permutations of $[n]$[n]. Consider the statistic counting weak excedances that are also mid-points of a decreasing subsequence of length $3$3 (Statistic $373$373), and let the cycle descent number be Statistic $317$317.\n\nWeak-excedance mid-point conjecture. The number of weak excedances that are also mid-points of a decreasing subsequence of length $3$3 (Statistic $373$373) is equidistributed with the cycle descent number (Statistic $317$317) and thus exhibits the cyclic sieving phenomenon under involutions with $2^{n-1}$2 n−1 fixed points.\n\nThis is stated among the paper's conjectured cyclic-sieving instances and was tested up to $n=10$n=10; the paper supplies no proof or resolution in the provided material.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Ashleigh Adams, Jennifer Elder, Nadia Lafrenière, Erin McNicholas, Jessica Striker and Amanda Welch, “Cyclic sieving on permutations – an analysis of maps and statistics in the FindStat database”, arXiv:2402.16251 (2025).\n\ncyclic-sievingpermutation-statistics\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 5 days ago\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeometries admitting trialities for the symmetric and alternating groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19230)\nciteturn3academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; In incidence geometry, a triality is a symmetry cyclically exchanging triples of types of elements. ... In this paper, we present two infinite families of flag transitive, thin and residually connected geometries admitting trialities and no dualities with type preserving automorphism groups isomorphic to Sym(n) or Alt(n).\nTitle: Geometries admitting trialities for the symmetric and alternating groups\nAuthors: Rémi Delaby\nDate: Tue Jul 21 16:02:43 2026\n\nIn incidence geometry, a triality is a symmetry cyclically exchanging triples of types of elements. Requiring geometries with trialities to satisfy standard regularity conditions makes their construction highly non trivial, and known examples are rare. In this paper, we present two infinite families of flag transitive, thin and residually connected geometries admitting trialities and no dualities with type preserving automorphism groups isomorphic to Sym(n) or Alt(n). We also develop general methods that extend to other settings. The residues of the two families are fundamentally different. In the first family, the maximal parabolics have constant size while in the other their size grows essentially linearly with the degree of the group.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrustration indices of signed subcubic graphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2511.15226v1)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last month; In this paper, we prove that the frustration index of an $n$-vertex signed connected simple subcubic graph, other than $(K_4, -)$, is at most $\\frac{3n + 2}{8}$, and we characterize the family of signed graphs for which this bound is attained.\n\n## Frustration indices of signed subcubic graphs\n\nSirui Chen, Jiaao Li, Zhouningxin Wang\n\nPublished: 2025-11-19\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\ncubic graph graph signed\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe frustration index of a signed graph is defined as the minimum number of negative edges among all switching-equivalent signatures. This can be regarded as a generalization of the classical \\textsc{Max-Cut} problem in graphs, as the \\textsc{Max-Cut} problem is equivalent to determining the frustration index of signed graphs with all edges being negative signs. In this paper, we prove that the frustration index of an $n$-vertex signed connected simple subcubic graph, other than $(K_4, -)$, is at most $\\frac{3n + 2}{8}$, and we characterize the family of signed graphs for which this bound is attained. This bound can be further improved to $\\frac{n}{3}$ for signed $2$-edge-connected simple subcubic graphs, with the exceptional signed graphs being characterized. As a corollary, every signed $2$-edge-connected simple cubic graph on at least $10$ vertices and with $m$ edges has its frustration index at most $\\frac{2}{9}m$, where the upper bound is tight as it is achieved by an infinite family of signed cubic graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[Literature Review] Frustration indices of signed subcubic graphs (https://www.themoonlight.io/en/review/frustration-indices-of-signed