hat no automorphism $\\theta$θ can exist that swaps two generators while fixing the third (e.g., $\\theta(\\rho_1) = \\rho_2, \\theta(\\rho_2) = \\rho_1, \\theta(\\rho_3) = \\rho_3$θ(ρ 1​)=ρ 2​,θ(ρ 2​)=ρ 1​,θ(ρ 3​)=ρ 3​) while preserving the geometric constraints of the specific vertex stabilizers in the associated 4-transitive action.\n\n### Two Families of Geometries\n\n  1. Trident Graphs ($n = 13 + 9k$n=13+9 k): These graphs are characterized by the property that the size of the maximal parabolic subgroups remains constant relative to $n$n. The automorphism group is $A_n$A n​ if $k$k is even and $S_n$S n​ if $k$k is odd.\n  2. Blooming Trident Graphs ($n = 16 + 6k$n=16+6 k): This family is distinguished by the fact that the size of the maximal parabolics grows linearly with $n$n. The automorphism group is $S_n$S n​.\n\n### Existence of Trialities and Dualities\n\nThe presence of trialities and the absence of dualities are proven via automorphisms of the group $G$G.\n\n  * Triality: A triality exists if there is an automorphism $\\theta \\in \\text{Aut}(G)$θ∈Aut(G) such that $\\theta(\\rho_i) = \\rho_{i \\pmod 3 + 1}$θ(ρ i​)=ρ i(mod 3)+1​. The authors identify specific permutations whose conjugation in $G$G cyclically permutes the generators, inducing a triality on the geometry.\n  * Absence of Duality: Dualities are shown to be impossible by proving that no automorphism $\\theta$θ can exist that swaps two generators while fixing the third (e.g., $\\theta(\\rho_1) = \\rho_2, \\theta(\\rho_2) = \\rho_1, \\theta(\\rho_3) = \\rho_3$θ(ρ 1​)=ρ 2​,θ(ρ 2​)=ρ 1​,θ(ρ 3​)=ρ 3​) while preserving the geometric constraints of the specific vertex stabilizers in the associated 4-transitive action.\n\nThe paper concludes that for $n \\geq 10$n≥10, such geometries appear to be common, and it provides specific conjectures regarding the construction of similar geometries for arbitrary $n$n, noting that cases for $n < 10$n<10 do not yield these symmetric properties.\n---------------